notes={
's01-jung-psyche':'C. G. Jung, “Psychology and Religion,” in Psychology and Religion: West and East, CW 11, trans. R. F. C. Hull, 2nd ed. (Princeton University Press, 1969; consulted second printing, 1973), ¶¶16, 18; digital PDF page 23. Source house `jung-1969-psychology-religion-cw11`, p007. The intervening examples concern the efficacy of psychic facts; this does not assert the physical world’s non-existence.',
's01-jung-god':'Jung, Psychology and Religion: West and East, CW 11, 2nd ed. (1969; consulted printing 1973), ¶137; digital PDF page 75. Source house `jung-1969-psychology-religion-cw11`, p008. This is the psychologically dominant value, distinct from the essay’s ontological God/Subject argument.',
's01-aion-shadow':'C. G. Jung, Aion: Researches into the Phenomenology of the Self, 2nd ed., trans. R. F. C. Hull, CW 9ii (Princeton University Press, 1979 paperback carrier), ¶¶14, 16, print pp.8–9; digital PDF pages 20–21. Existing house `jung-1978-aion-cw9-2`, p017. The selected 1978 hardcover identity remains distinct from this consulted 1979 carrier; no independent equivalence is claimed.',
's01-jung-paradox':'C. G. Jung, Psychology and Alchemy, CW 12, trans. R. F. C. Hull, 2nd ed., completely revised 1968 (Princeton University Press, first paperback printing 1980), ¶¶18–19; digital PDF pages 47–48. Source house `jung-1980-psychology-alchemy-cw12`, p001. The need for cultivation and danger of misunderstood paradox are retained in the source context.',
's01-jung-logos-eros':'C. G. Jung, Mysterium Coniunctionis, CW 14, trans. R. F. C. Hull, 2nd ed., copyright 1970 (Princeton University Press, first paperback printing 1977), ¶224; digital PDF page 133. Source house `jung-1977-mysterium-coniunctionis-cw14`, p001. ¶225 discusses counterexamples; Jung’s historical gendered account is distinct from the essay’s two-operation continuation.',
's01-golden-flower':'C. G. Jung, “Commentary on The Secret of the Golden Flower,” in Alchemical Studies, CW 13, trans. R. F. C. Hull (Princeton University Press, first paperback printing 1983; copyright 1967), ¶20, print p.16; digital PDF page 30. Source house `jung-1983-alchemical-studies-cw13`, p001. The preceding warning against a mechanical recipe is retained; this is Jung’s commentary rather than the primary Chinese text.',
's01-numinous':'Jung, Psychology and Religion: West and East, CW 11, 2nd ed. (1969; consulted printing 1973), ¶6; digital PDF pages 19–20. Source house `jung-1969-psychology-religion-cw11`, p009, locates the Otto-derived account. The separate feeling-toned-complex lead in “A Review of the Complex Theory,” CW 8, is not newly located by this card.',
's01-jung-know':'The 1959 BBC Face to Face interview with John Freeman and the January 1960 Listener letter remain separate source acquisitions. The second current quotation is located in Jung, The Symbolic Life, CW 18, trans. R. F. C. Hull (Princeton University Press, 1976), ¶1589, “Jung and Religious Belief,” question 3; digital PDF page 654. Source house `jung-1976-symbolic-life-cw18`, p001. The answer separates personal conviction from scientific proof. It cannot certify the interview or Listener wording.',
's01-jung-individuation':'Jung, Psychological Types, CW 6, Hull revision of H. G. Baynes, first corrected Princeton/Bollingen paperback printing 1976, ¶758; digital PDF page 409. Source house `jung-1976-psychological-types-cw6`, p001. Also Jung, The Archetypes and the Collective Unconscious, CW 9i, 2nd ed., trans. R. F. C. Hull (Routledge, consulted carrier n.d.), ¶490, print p.275; digital PDF page 286. Source house `jung-cw9i-hull-routledge-second-edition`, p002. The first rejects isolation; the second distinguishes the ego from psychological totality.',
's01-jung-art':'Jung, “On the Relation of Analytical Psychology to Poetry,” The Spirit in Man, Art, and Literature, CW 15, trans. R. F. C. Hull (Princeton University Press, first paperback printing 1971; copyright 1966), ¶130; digital PDF page 74. Source house `jung-1971-spirit-man-art-literature-cw15`, p001. The contemporary cultural application and typological artist reading remain the essay’s.',
's01-mana':'Jung, “On Psychic Energy,” The Structure and Dynamics of the Psyche, CW 8, trans. R. F. C. Hull, 2nd ed., copyright 1969 (Princeton University Press, third corrected printing 1975), ¶52 n.44; digital PDF page 75. Source house `jung-1975-structure-dynamics-psyche-cw8`, p002. This is a Codrington-mediated witness. The separate n.123 wording remains to be collated; this card does not silently certify it.',
's01-lucifer':'Jung, Aion, CW 9ii, 2nd ed., trans. R. F. C. Hull (Princeton University Press, consulted paperback carrier 1979), ¶127, print p.72; digital PDF page 84. Existing house `jung-1978-aion-cw9-2`, p021, now records the shared Morning Star symbol. The draft’s additional ¶68 science/technology phrase, independent Isaiah/Vulgate textual collation, and Shelley lead are not newly certified by this card.',
's01-privatio':'Jung, Aion, CW 9ii (consulted paperback carrier 1979), ¶¶74–75, print pp.41–42; digital PDF pages 53–54. Existing house `jung-1978-aion-cw9-2`, p018. Jung specifies the empirical-psychological register; theological and metaphysical endorsement are distinct claims.',
's01-aion-antichrist':'Jung, Aion, CW 9ii (consulted paperback carrier 1979), ¶¶76–78, print pp.42–43; digital PDF pages 54–55. Existing house `jung-1978-aion-cw9-2`, p019. The symbolic shadow and counterstroke are located together; this is Jung’s historical-theological reconstruction.',
's01-antimimon':'Jung, Aion, CW 9ii (consulted paperback carrier 1979), continuation of ¶75, print p.42; digital PDF page 54, locates the Antichrist as imitating spirit. Existing house `jung-1978-aion-cw9-2`, p020. The distinct contemporary false-spirit description is ¶67, print p.35; digital PDF page 47. Cite each in its own context; the current M05 Antichrist quotation points to ¶75.',
's01-jung-symbol':'Jung, Psychological Types, CW 6, Hull revision of H. G. Baynes, first corrected Princeton/Bollingen paperback printing 1976, ¶814, Definition 51, “Symbol”; digital PDF page 430. Source house `jung-1976-psychological-types-cw6`, p002. The semiotic badge/token examples retain the contrast between a known referent and the relatively unknown fact formulated by a symbol.',
's01-jung-archetype':'Jung, The Structure and Dynamics of the Psyche, CW 8, 2nd ed., third corrected printing 1975, ¶417, print pp.213–214; digital PDF pages 225–226. Source house `jung-1975-structure-dynamics-psyche-cw8`, p001. The two-irrepresentables sentence is still ¶417; ¶418 begins at digital page 227, print p.215. Also Jung, The Archetypes and the Collective Unconscious, CW 9i, Hull translation, Routledge second-edition carrier n.d., ¶155, print p.79; digital PDF page 90. Source house `jung-cw9i-hull-routledge-second-edition`, p001. The formal possibility is not an inherited determinate image, and the psychoid comparison remains Jung’s analogy/hypothesis.',
's01-jung-self':'Jung, Aion, CW 9ii (consulted paperback carrier 1979), ch.V n.74, print p.61; digital PDF page 73. Existing house `jung-1978-aion-cw9-2`, p022. The reply to Victor White concerns psychological unity of the Self; the essay’s Śaiva/QL comparison remains its own continuation.',
's01-jung-quaternity':'Jung, Psychology and Religion: West and East, CW 11, 2nd ed. (1969; consulted printing 1973), ¶246; digital PDF page 140. Source house `jung-1969-psychology-religion-cw11`, p010. Also Jung, Psychology and Alchemy, CW 12, 2nd ed., consulted paperback printing 1980, ¶31; digital PDF page 56. Source house `jung-1980-psychology-alchemy-cw12`, p002. Four determinants and vacillation between three and four are located; QL’s 4+2 is the essay’s continuation.',
's01-jung-five':'Jung, Mysterium Coniunctionis, CW 14, 2nd ed., consulted paperback printing 1977, ¶439; digital PDF page 233. Source house `jung-1977-mysterium-coniunctionis-cw14`, p002. The square/circle/quintessence passage belongs to commentary on Ripley’s vessel and son; QL #5 and #5→#0 are native continuation.'}
